#import "/shared/book.typ": *
#let p = problem.with(code: "MM8036", unit: "M2")
#let items = (
p(1,"E","U1–U3 Substitution",[Reduce $(lambda x.lambda z.x z)y$ and give its type under $y:A->B$.],[No binder conflicts with the argument.],[The resulting abstraction applies $y$.],[The reduction gives $lambda z.y z$. Under $z:A$, application yields $y z:B$, so abstraction gives $A->B$ in the context $y:A->B$.],[Supply the context rather than treating the free $y$ as closed.]),
p(2,"E","U5–U6 Identity",[Give the type and defining reflexive case of transport in a family $P:A->U_0$.],[Specify both endpoints.],[At reflexivity it is the identity function.],[For $a,b:A$ and $p:"Id"_A(a,b)$, transport has type $P(a)->P(b)$. Its reflexive clause sends $u:P(a)$ to $u$. Path induction extends this clause to arbitrary $p$.],[Include the direction and the role of path induction.]),
p(3,"C","U4–U5 Constructive evidence",[Construct a term taking $Sigma_(x:A)(B(x) times C(x))$ to $(Sigma_(x:A)B(x)) times(Sigma_(x:A)C(x))$. Explain why the reverse direction is generally unavailable.],[Use the same witness in both output pairs.],[Two independent existential witnesses need not coincide.],[Send $(a,(b,c))$ to $((a,b),(a,c))$. In the reverse direction the two supplied witnesses may differ. For a set-model counterexample, let $A=\{0,1\}$, let $B$ be inhabited only at zero and $C$ only at one. Both separate dependent sums are inhabited, while no index supports both fibers, so the original Sigma is empty. Soundness rules out a uniform reverse construction for all such families.],[Give the term and a concrete model showing the missing common witness.]),
p(4,"C","U6 Induction",[Prove that mapping the identity function over a vector returns the original vector, using propositional equality.],[Induct on the vector.],[Use congruence of the fixed-head cons constructor.],[At nil, map computes to nil and reflexivity proves the equality. At $"cons"(a,v)$, map computes to $"cons"(a,"map"("id",v))$. The induction hypothesis identifies its tail with $v$; congruence under $w mapsto "cons"(a,w)$ gives equality with $"cons"(a,v)$. The result lives in the same length-indexed vector type on both sides.],[Preserve the index and identify the congruence function.]),
p(5,"A","U2–U4 Confluence and typing",[Suppose a closed simply typed term reduces along two paths to $u,v$. Prove $u,v$ have a common normal form of the original type.],[Use preservation, normalization, and confluence in different roles.],[Normalize both reducts and then compare them.],[Preservation gives both reducts the original type; closedness is retained by reduction. Strong normalization gives normal reducts $n_u,n_v$. Both are normal forms of the original source, so confluence forces them to be alpha equivalent. Repeated preservation gives that common normal form the original type. The argument uses confluence for agreement and normalization for existence; neither theorem substitutes for the other.],[State the role of all three metatheorems.]),
p(6,"A","U6–U8 Equality audit",[Explain why the following inference is invalid: “The set model identifies all proofs of equality; therefore the paper theory may use K, and the labs may omit `--without-K`.” Give a correct relationship among syntax, semantics, and the lab options.],[Soundness is one-way, and checker options define the accepted fragment.],[A model can validate additional principles that the syntax does not assume.],[Semantic identification does not reflect back to a syntactic derivation without a suitable completeness theorem. Our intensional syntax uses J and has not assumed K. The set model validates the chosen rules but may forget distinctions between proofs. The labs deliberately check a corresponding restricted fragment with `--without-K`; omitting that flag would change the allowed pattern-matching discipline and would no longer certify the stated course constraint. Their existing proofs do pass with the restriction.],[Identify the failed converse and distinguish changing a checker option from proving a new theorem.]),
)
